{"abstract":"Causality plays an important role in understanding intelligent behavior, and there is a wealth of literature on mathematical models for causality, most of which is focused on causal graphs. Causal graphs are a powerful tool for a wide range of applications, in particular when the relevant variables are known and at the same level of abstraction. However, the given variables can also be unstructured data, like pixels of an image. Meanwhile, the causal variables, such as the positions of objects in the image, can be arbitrary deterministic functions of the given variables. Moreover, the causal variables may form a hierarchy of abstractions, in which the macro-level variables are deterministic functions of the micro-level variables. Causal graphs are limited when it comes to modeling this kind of situation. In the presence of deterministic relationships there is generally no causal graph that satisfies both the Markov condition and the faithfulness condition. We introduce factored space models as an alternative to causal graphs which naturally represent both probabilistic and deterministic relationships at all levels of abstraction. Moreover, we introduce structural independence and establish that it is equivalent to statistical independence in every distribution that factorizes over the factored space. This theorem generalizes the classical soundness and completeness theorem for d-separation.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Garrabrant, Scott","Mayer, Matthias Georg","Wache, Magdalena","Lang, Leon","Eisenstat, Sam","Dell, Holger"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-776391"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Netherlands","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Machine Intelligence Research Institute","University of Amsterdam"],"keyword":["Abstraction"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"und","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"factored space models towards causality between levels of abstraction","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dpr","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/107627229/2412.02579v1.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},"id":"S01-000.00-776391","ids":["itu-3d1a7e6f-9b36-4a6d-9e9f-3bb4e7094941"],"keyword":["abstraction","deterministic causality","factored space models","structural independence"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-83AE74C365C4F09790EF97664B1A1CD0"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/107627229/2412.02579v1.pdf","version":"Pre-print"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"us","key":"machine intelligence research institute","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Machine Intelligence Research Institute","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"nl","key":"university of amsterdam","loc":["Netherlands","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Amsterdam","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Algorithms"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Scott","key":"garrabrantpau","last":"Garrabrant","name":"Garrabrant, Scott","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Matthias Georg","key":"mayerpau","last":"Mayer","name":"Mayer, Matthias Georg","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Magdalena","key":"wachepau","last":"Wache","name":"Wache, Magdalena","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Leon","key":"langpau","last":"Lang","name":"Lang, Leon","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Sam","key":"eisenstatpau","last":"Eisenstat","name":"Eisenstat, Sam","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Holger","key":"dellpau","last":"Dell","locid":"ef9b447a-d6c4-4250-a506-093730db84d6","name":"Dell, Holger","orcid":"0000-0001-8955-0786","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Undetermined","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-3d1a7e6f-9b36-4a6d-9e9f-3bb4e7094941"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Factored space models : Towards causality between levels of abstraction","type":"Preprint","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-776391","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Graph representation learning is central for the application of machine learning (ML) models to complex graphs, such as social networks. Ensuring `fair' representations is essential, due to the societal implications and the use of sensitive personal data. In this paper, we demonstrate how the parametrization of the \\emph{CrossWalk} algorithm influences the ability to infer a sensitive attributes from node embeddings. By fine-tuning hyperparameters, we show that it is possible to either significantly enhance or obscure the detectability of these attributes. This functionality offers a valuable tool for improving the fairness of ML systems utilizing graph embeddings, making them adaptable to different fairness paradigms.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Vetter, Dennis","Forth, Jasper","Roig, Gemma","Dell, Holger"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-776408"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Goethe University Frankfurt"],"keyword":["cs CY","cs SI"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"und","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"fairness through controlled un awareness in node embeddings","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dpr","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-SA","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/107039036/2407.20024v1.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-776408","ids":["itu-66c9f1e9-1321-4d04-b93b-ae8662eab5bb"],"keyword":["cs.CY","cs.SI"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-B5CB3356C3BAD3195A414E4030FA307B"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-SA","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/107039036/2407.20024v1.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-SA","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2407.20024%20Focus%20to%20learn%20more","version":"Pre-print"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"de","key":"goethe university frankfurt","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Goethe University Frankfurt","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Algorithms"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Dennis","key":"vetterpau","last":"Vetter","name":"Vetter, Dennis","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jasper","key":"forthpau","last":"Forth","name":"Forth, Jasper","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Gemma","key":"roigpau","last":"Roig","name":"Roig, Gemma","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Holger","key":"dellpau","last":"Dell","locid":"ef9b447a-d6c4-4250-a506-093730db84d6","name":"Dell, Holger","orcid":"0000-0001-8955-0786","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2407.20024focustolearnmore","pages":"8","publisher":"arXiv","status":"p"},"review":"Undetermined","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-66c9f1e9-1321-4d04-b93b-ae8662eab5bb"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Fairness Through Controlled (Un)Awareness in Node Embeddings","type":"Preprint","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-776408","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"We present a randomized algorithm for solving low-degree polynomial equation systems over finite fields faster than exhaustive search. In order to do so, we follow a line of work by Lokshtanov, Paturi, Tamaki, Williams, and Yu (SODA 2017), Bj\\\"orklund, Kaski, and Williams (ICALP 2019), and Dinur (SODA 2021). In particular, we generalize Dinur's algorithm for $\\mathbb{F}_2$ to all finite fields, in particular the \"symbolic interpolation\" of Bj\\\"orklund, Kaski, and Williams, and we use an efficient trimmed multipoint evaluation and interpolation procedure for multivariate polynomials over finite fields by Van der Hoeven and Schost (AAECC 2013). The running time of our algorithm matches that of Dinur's algorithm for $\\mathbb{F}_2$ and is significantly faster than the one of Lokshtanov et al. for $q>2$. We complement our results with tight conditional lower bounds that, surprisingly, we were not able to find in the literature. In particular, under the strong exponential time hypothesis, we prove that it is impossible to solve $n$-variate low-degree polynomial equation systems over $\\mathbb{F}_q$ in time $O((q-\\varepsilon)^{n})$. As a bonus, we show that under the counting version of the strong exponential time hypothesis, it is impossible to compute the number of roots of a single $n$-variate low-degree polynomial over $\\mathbb{F}_q$ in time ${O((q-\\varepsilon)^{n})}$; this generalizes a result of Williams (SOSA 2018) from $\\mathbb{F}_2$ to all finite fields.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Dell, Holger","Haak, Anselm","Kallmayer, Melvin","Wennmann, Leo"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-776428"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany","Netherlands"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Goethe University Frankfurt","Maastricht University","Paderborn University"],"keyword":["cs CC","cs DS"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"und","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"solving polynomial equations over finite fields","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dpr","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2410.20162%20Focus%20to%20learn%20more%20Submission%20history","version":"Pre-print"},"id":"S01-000.00-776428","ids":["itu-97d0f84f-f56f-4680-942a-b404e12b8760"],"keyword":["cs.CC","cs.DS"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-E2E1C006DE29B4A130E87FDD2CA53369"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/107038968/2410.20162v1.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2410.20162%20Focus%20to%20learn%20more%20Submission%20history","version":"Pre-print"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Algorithms"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"de","key":"goethe university frankfurt","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Goethe University Frankfurt","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"de","key":"paderborn university","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Paderborn University","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"nl","key":"maastricht university","loc":["Netherlands","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Maastricht University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Holger","key":"dellpau","last":"Dell","locid":"ef9b447a-d6c4-4250-a506-093730db84d6","name":"Dell, Holger","orcid":"0000-0001-8955-0786","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Anselm","key":"haakpau","last":"Haak","name":"Haak, Anselm","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Melvin","key":"kallmayerpau","last":"Kallmayer","name":"Kallmayer, Melvin","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Leo","key":"wennmannpau","last":"Wennmann","name":"Wennmann, Leo","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2410.20162focustolearnmoresubmissionhistory","pages":"25","publisher":"arXiv","status":"p"},"review":"Undetermined","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-97d0f84f-f56f-4680-942a-b404e12b8760"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Solving Polynomial Equations Over Finite Fields","type":"Preprint","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-776428","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"In this paper, we design efficient algorithms to approximately count the number of edges of a given k-hypergraph, and to sample an approximately uniform random edge. The hypergraph is not given explicitly, and can be accessed only through its colourful independence oracle: The colourful independence oracle returns yes or no depending on whether a given subset of the vertices contains an edge that is colourful with respect to a given vertex-colouring. Our results extend and/or strengthen recent results in the graph oracle literature due to Beame et al. (ITCS 2018), Dell and Lapinskas (STOC 2018), and Bhattacharya et al. (ISAAC 2019). Our results have consequences for approximate counting/sampling: We can turn certain kinds of decision algorithms into approximate counting/sampling algorithms without causing much overhead in the running time. We apply this approximate-counting/sampling-to-decision reduction to key problems in fine-grained complexity (such as k-SUM, k-OV and weighted k-Clique) and parameterised complexity (such as induced subgraphs of size k or weight-k solutions to CSPs).","author_count":3,"contributors":["Dell, Holger","Lapinskas, John","Meeks, Kitty"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382388"],"filters":{"added":"2022-12","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Bristol","University of Glasgow"],"keyword":["Approximate counting","Fine grained complexity","colourful independence oracle","k hypergraph","uniform edge sampling"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"approximately counting and sampling small witnesses using a colorful decision oracle","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-SA","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/90409745/1907.04826.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382388","identifier":[{"id":"85135704226","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-4b051a07-c184-4915-aca3-b1bb154ece01"],"keyword":["approximate counting","colourful independence oracle","fine-grained complexity","k-hypergraph","uniform edge sampling"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-C866D86170D61BE42AB580689FE176B0"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-SA","type":"rem","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.04826","version":"Authors Final Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-SA","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/90409745/1907.04826.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Algorithms"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"university of bristol","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Bristol","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"gb","key":"university of glasgow","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Glasgow","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Holger","key":"dellpau","last":"Dell","locid":"ef9b447a-d6c4-4250-a506-093730db84d6","name":"Dell, Holger","orcid":"0000-0001-8955-0786","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"John","key":"lapinskaspau","last":"Lapinskas","name":"Lapinskas, John","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Kitty","key":"meekspau","last":"Meeks","name":"Meeks, Kitty","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1137/19m130604x","issn":["00975397","10957111"],"issn_org":["00975397"],"issue":"4","journal":"SIAM Journal on Computing","pages":"849-899","publisher":"Society for Industrial and Applied Mathematics","status":"p","title":"SIAM Journal on Computing","title_org":"S I A M Journal on Computing","volume":"51"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-4b051a07-c184-4915-aca3-b1bb154ece01"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Approximately Counting and Sampling Small Witnesses Using a Colorful Decision Oracle","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382388","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"We study a query model of computation in which an n-vertex k-hypergraph can be accessed only via its independence oracle or via its colourful independence oracle, and each oracle query may incur a cost depending on the size of the query. In each of these models, we obtain oracle algorithms to approximately count the hypergraph's edges, and we unconditionally prove that no oracle algorithm for this problem can have significantly smaller worst-case oracle cost than our algorithms.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Dell, Holger","Lapinskas, John","Meeks, Kitty"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-776450"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany","United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Frankfurt University of Applied Sciences","University of Bristol","University of Glasgow"],"keyword":["Approximate counting","Fine grained complexity","Hypergraphs","cs CC","cs DS"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"und","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"nearly optimal independence oracle algorithms for edge estimation in hypergraphs","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dpr","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2211.03874","version":"Pre-print"},"funding_acknowledgement":["Kitty Meeks: Supported by EPSRC grant EP/V032305/1."],"id":"S01-000.00-776450","ids":["itu-d1aca994-848a-49ed-985c-a0052d51930b"],"keyword":["Approximate counting","Fine-grained complexity,","Graph oracles","Hypergraphs","cs.CC","cs.DS"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-C4761588211D1457CEB52C458B517CE3"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/106959427/2211.03874v2.pdf","version":"Other Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2211.03874","version":"Pre-print"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Algorithms"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"de","key":"frankfurt university of applied sciences","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Frankfurt University of Applied Sciences","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"gb","key":"university of bristol","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Bristol","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"gb","key":"university of glasgow","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Glasgow","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Holger","key":"dellpau","last":"Dell","locid":"ef9b447a-d6c4-4250-a506-093730db84d6","name":"Dell, Holger","orcid":"0000-0001-8955-0786","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"John","key":"lapinskaspau","last":"Lapinskas","name":"Lapinskas, John","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Kitty","key":"meekspau","last":"Meeks","name":"Meeks, Kitty","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2211.03874","pages":"92","status":"p"},"review":"Undetermined","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-d1aca994-848a-49ed-985c-a0052d51930b"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Nearly optimal independence oracle algorithms for edge estimation in hypergraphs","type":"Preprint","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-776450","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"We systematically investigate the complexity of counting subgraph patterns modulo fixed integers. For example, it is known that the parity of the number of k-matchings can be determined in polynomial time by a simple reduction to the determinant. We generalize this to an nf(t,s)-time algorithm to compute modulo 2t the number of subgraph occurrences of patterns that are s vertices away from being matchings. This shows that the known polynomial-time cases of subgraph detection (Jansen and Marx, SODA 2015) carry over into the setting of counting modulo 2t. Complementing our algorithm, we also give a simple and self-contained proof that counting k-matchings modulo odd integers q is ModqW[1]-complete and prove that counting k-paths modulo 2 is ⊕W[1]-complete, answering an open question by Björklund, Dell, and Husfeldt (ICALP 2015).","author_count":3,"contributors":["Curticapean, Radu-Cristian","Dell, Holger","Husfeldt, Thore"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384405"],"event":{"acronym":"ESA","title":"European Symposium on Algorithms"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Complexity theory","Counting subgraph patterns","Modulo computations","Parameterized complexity","Polynomial time algorithms"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"modular counting of subgraphs matchings matching splittable graphs and paths","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.ESA.2021.34","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384405","identifier":[{"id":"85115050589","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-d51dcde5-ec04-4d8f-afcb-d15635254bfd"],"keyword":["Complexity theory","Counting subgraph patterns","Modulo computations","Parameterized complexity","Polynomial-time algorithms"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-0C430DCB61086314B3D7E9F4C1AE1CBF"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.ESA.2021.34","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86408107/LIPIcs_ESA_2021_34.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Algorithms"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Radu-Cristian","key":"curticapeanpau","last":"Curticapean","locid":"53c45a58-4c3c-424c-80a2-c39e733fd7c3","name":"Curticapean, Radu-Cristian","orcid":"0000-0001-7201-9905","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Holger","key":"dellpau","last":"Dell","locid":"ef9b447a-d6c4-4250-a506-093730db84d6","name":"Dell, Holger","orcid":"0000-0001-8955-0786","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Thore","key":"husfeldtpau","last":"Husfeldt","locid":"472021c7-73cc-44ba-a4ef-70cd2bed8601","name":"Husfeldt, Thore","orcid":"0000-0001-9078-4512","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.4230/lipics.esa.2021.34","publisher":"Schloss Dagstuhl - Leibniz-Zentrum fuer Informatik GmbH","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the 29th Annual European Symposium on Algorithms","volume":"204"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85115050589"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-d51dcde5-ec04-4d8f-afcb-d15635254bfd"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Modular Counting of Subgraphs: Matchings, Matching-Splittable Graphs, and Paths","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384405","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"In this article, we introduce a general framework for fine-grained reductions of approximate counting problems to their decision versions. (Thus, we use an oracle that decides whether any witness exists to multiplicatively approximate the number of witnesses with minimal overhead.) This mirrors a foundational result of Sipser (STOC 1983) and Stockmeyer (SICOMP 1985) in the polynomial-time setting, and a similar result of Müller (IWPEC 2006) in the FPT setting. Using our framework, we obtain such reductions for some of the most important problems in fine-grained complexity: the Orthogonal Vectors problem, 3SUM, and the Negative-Weight Triangle problem (which is closely related to All-Pairs Shortest Path). While all these problems have simple algorithms over which it is conjectured that no polynomial improvement is possible, our reductions would remain interesting even if these conjectures were proved; they have only polylogarithmic overhead and can therefore be applied to subpolynomial improvements such as the n3/ exp(Θ (√ log n))-time algorithm for the Negative-Weight Triangle problem due to Williams (STOC 2014). Our framework is also general enough to apply to versions of the problems for which more efficient algorithms are known. For example, the Orthogonal Vectors problem over GF(m)d for constant m can be solved in time n · poly (d) by a result of Williams and Yu (SODA 2014); our result implies that we can approximately count the number of orthogonal pairs with essentially the same running time. We also provide a fine-grained reduction from approximate #SAT to SAT. Suppose the Strong Exponential Time Hypothesis (SETH) is false, so that for some 1 < c < 2 and all k there is an O(cn)-time algorithm for k-SAT. Then we prove that for all k, there is an O(( c + o(1))n)-time algorithm for approximate #k-SAT. In particular, our result implies that the Exponential Time Hypothesis (ETH) is equivalent to the seemingly weaker statement that there is no algorithm to approximate #3-SAT to within a factor of 1+ɛ in time 2o(n)/ ɛ2 (taking ɛ > 0 as part of the input).","author_count":2,"contributors":["Dell, Holger","Lapinskas, John"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381964"],"filters":{"added":"2022-03","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Bristol"],"keyword":["Approximate counting","Fine grained complexity","Satisfiability"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"fine grained reductions from approximate counting to decision","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-SA","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86408155/1707.04609.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-381964","identifier":[{"id":"85108024706","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-2f308edc-1bf7-4546-9917-3ad656594f47"],"keyword":["Approximate Counting","Fine-grained complexity","Satisfiability"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-D0491B62BA99BF6A91BC3C6D169E7DCF"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-SA","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86408155/1707.04609.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Algorithms"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"university of bristol","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Bristol","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Holger","key":"dellpau","last":"Dell","locid":"ef9b447a-d6c4-4250-a506-093730db84d6","name":"Dell, Holger","orcid":"0000-0001-8955-0786","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"John","key":"lapinskaspau","last":"Lapinskas","name":"Lapinskas, John","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3442352","issn":["19423454","19423462"],"issn_org":["19423454"],"issue":"2","journal":"ACM Transactions on Computation Theory","pages":"1-24","paperid":"8","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"ACM Transactions on Computation Theory","volume":"13"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-2f308edc-1bf7-4546-9917-3ad656594f47"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Fine-Grained Reductions from Approximate Counting to Decision","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381964","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"In this paper, we prove “black box” results for turning algorithms which decide whether or not a witness exists into algorithms to approximately count the number of witnesses, or to sample from the set of witnesses approximately uniformly, with essentially the same running time. We do so by extending the framework of Dell and Lapinskas (STOC 2018), which covers decision problems that can be expressed as edge detection in bipartite graphs given limited oracle access; our framework covers problems which can be expressed as edge detection in arbitrary k-hypergraphs given limited oracle access. (Simulating this oracle generally corresponds to invoking a decision algorithm.) This includes many key problems in both the fine-grained setting (such as k-SUM, k-OV and weighted k-Clique) and the parameterised setting (such as induced subgraphs of size k or weight-k solutions to CSPs). From an algorithmic standpoint, our results will make the development of new approximate counting algorithms substantially easier; indeed, it already yields a new state-of-the-art algorithm for approximately counting graph motifs, improving on Jerrum and Meeks (JCSS 2015) unless the input graph is very dense and the desired motif very small. Our k-hypergraph reduction framework generalises and strengthens results in the graph oracle literature due to Beame et al. (ITCS 2018) and Bhattacharya et al. (CoRR abs/1808.00691). Read More: https://epubs.siam.org/doi/10.1137/1.9781611975994.135","author_count":4,"contributors":["Dell, Holger","Lapinskas, John","Meeks, Kitty"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383915"],"event":{"acronym":"SODA","title":"ACM-SIAM Symposium on Discrete Algorithms"},"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Bristol","University of Glasgow"],"keyword":["Approximate counting","Fine grained complexity","Sampling algorithms","k hypergraphs","oracle access"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"approximately counting and sampling small witnesses using a colourful decision oracle","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85551356/1907.04826.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383915","identifier":[{"id":"85084066587","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-b5b5c17f-c4e2-4969-aa5d-d99cf720266c"],"keyword":["approximate counting","fine-grained complexity","k-hypergraphs","oracle access","sampling algorithms"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-910D1E87985F1A7F8A88A5B028ACB925"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85551356/1907.04826.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-SA","type":"rem","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.04826","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Algorithms"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"university of bristol","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Bristol","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"gb","key":"university of glasgow","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Glasgow","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Holger","key":"dellpau","last":"Dell","locid":"ef9b447a-d6c4-4250-a506-093730db84d6","name":"Dell, Holger","orcid":"0000-0001-8955-0786","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"John","key":"lapinskaspau","last":"Lapinskas","name":"Lapinskas, John","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Kitty","key":"meekspau","last":"Meeks","name":"Meeks, Kitty","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Shuchi","key":"chawlapedh","last":"Chawla","name":"Chawla, Shuchi","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1137/1.9781611975994.135","eisbn":["9781611975994"],"pages":"2201-2211","publisher":"Society for Industrial and Applied Mathematics","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the 2020 ACM-SIAM Symposium on Discrete Algorithms"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85084066587"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-b5b5c17f-c4e2-4969-aa5d-d99cf720266c"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Approximately counting and sampling small witnesses using a colourful decision oracle","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383915","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Many graph parameters can be expressed as homomorphism counts to fixed target graphs; this includes the number of independent sets and the number of k-colorings for any fixed k. Dyer and Greenhill (RSA 2000) gave a sweeping complexity dichotomy for such problems, classifying which target graphs render the problem polynomial-time solvable or #P-hard. In this paper, we give a new and shorter proof of this theorem, with previously unknown tight lower bounds under the exponential-time hypothesis. We similarly strengthen complexity dichotomies by Focke, Goldberg, and Živný (SODA 2018) for counting surjective homomorphisms to fixed graphs. Both results crucially rely on our main contribution, a complexity dichotomy for evaluating linear combinations of homomorphism numbers to fixed graphs. In the terminology of Lovász (Colloquium Publications 2012), this amounts to counting homomorphisms to quantum graphs.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Chen, Hubie","Curticapean, Radu-Cristian","Dell, Holger"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383410"],"event":{"acronym":"WG","title":"International Workshop on Graph-Theoretic Concepts in Computer Science"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Birkbeck, University of London"],"keyword":["Complexity dichotomy","Exponential time hypothesis","Homomorphism counts","P hard","Quantum graphs"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the exponential time complexity of counting quantum graph homomorphisms","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/84775049/postprint.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383410","identifier":[{"id":"85072855542","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-91ddb7bc-d6e0-4102-936a-80682b0242f0"],"keyword":["#P-hard","Complexity dichotomy","Exponential-time hypothesis","Homomorphism counts","Quantum graphs"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-B8058C980829AA17A2BD7FD788FB57CF"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/84775049/postprint.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"gb","key":"birkbeck university of london","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"Birkbeck, University of London","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Algorithms"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Hubie","key":"chenpau","last":"Chen","name":"Chen, Hubie","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Radu-Cristian","key":"curticapeanpau","last":"Curticapean","locid":"53c45a58-4c3c-424c-80a2-c39e733fd7c3","name":"Curticapean, Radu-Cristian","orcid":"0000-0001-7201-9905","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Holger","key":"dellpau","last":"Dell","locid":"ef9b447a-d6c4-4250-a506-093730db84d6","name":"Dell, Holger","orcid":"0000-0001-8955-0786","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-030-30786-8_28","eisbn":["9783030307868"],"isbn":["9783030307851"],"issn":["03029743"],"pages":"364-378","publisher":"Springer","series":"Lecture Notes in Computer Science","status":"p","subtitle":"45th International Workshop, WG 2019, Vall de Núria, Spain, June 19–21, 2019, Revised Papers","title":"Graph-Theoretic Concepts in Computer Science"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85072855542"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-91ddb7bc-d6e0-4102-936a-80682b0242f0"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"The Exponential-Time Complexity of Counting (Quantum) Graph Homomorphisms","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383410","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"The problem of (approximately) counting the independent sets of a bipartite graph (#BIS) is the canonical approximate counting problem that is complete in the intermediate complexity class #RH\\Pi_1. It is believed that #BIS does not have an efficient approximation algorithm but also that it is not NP-hard. We study the robustness of the intermediate complexity of #BIS by considering variants of the problem parameterised by the size of the independent set. We exhaustively map the complexity landscape for three problems, with respect to exact computation and approximation and with respect to conventional and parameterised complexity. The three problems are counting independent sets of a given size, counting independent sets with a given number of vertices in one vertex class and counting maximum independent sets amongst those with a given number of vertices in one vertex class. Among other things, we show that all of these problems are NP-hard to approximate within any polynomial ratio. (This is surprising because the corresponding problems without the size parameter are complete in #RH\\Pi_1, and hence are not believed to be NP-hard.) We also show that the first problem is #W[1]-hard to solve exactly but admits an FPTRAS, whereas the other two are W[1]-hard to approximate even within any polynomial ratio. Finally, we show that, when restricted to graphs of bounded degree, all three problems have efficient exact fixed-parameter algorithms.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Curticapean, Radu-Cristian","Dell, Holger","Fomin, Fedor","Goldberg, Leslie Ann","Lapinskas, John"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383211"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Norway","United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Bergen","University of Bristol","University of Oxford"],"keyword":["Approximate counting","Independent sets","Parameterised complexity"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a fixed parameter perspective on bis","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/84707589/1702.05543.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383211","identifier":[{"id":"85069489213","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-83f210ce-fa08-4999-8ffb-c207482d4888"],"keyword":["Approximate counting","Independent sets","Parameterised complexity"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-C58DDD06EAC52026AEC2C6DA7AB6CF28"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/84707589/1702.05543.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"https://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/license.html","type":"rem","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1702.05543","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Algorithms"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"no","key":"university of bergen","loc":["Norway","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"University of Bergen","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"gb","key":"university of oxford","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Oxford","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"gb","key":"university of bristol","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Bristol","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Radu-Cristian","key":"curticapeanpau","last":"Curticapean","locid":"53c45a58-4c3c-424c-80a2-c39e733fd7c3","name":"Curticapean, Radu-Cristian","orcid":"0000-0001-7201-9905","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Holger","key":"dellpau","last":"Dell","locid":"ef9b447a-d6c4-4250-a506-093730db84d6","name":"Dell, Holger","orcid":"0000-0001-8955-0786","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Fedor","key":"fominpau","last":"Fomin","name":"Fomin, Fedor","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Leslie Ann","key":"goldbergpau","last":"Goldberg","name":"Goldberg, Leslie Ann","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"John","key":"lapinskaspau","last":"Lapinskas","name":"Lapinskas, John","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/s00453-019-00606-4","issn":["01784617","14320541"],"issn_org":["01784617"],"issue":"10","journal":"Algorithmica","pages":"3844-3864","publisher":"Springer","status":"p","title":"Algorithmica","volume":"81"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85069489213"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-83f210ce-fa08-4999-8ffb-c207482d4888"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"A Fixed-Parameter Perspective on #BIS","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383211","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"We devise an algorithm that approximately computes the number of paths of length k in a given directed graph with n vertices up to a multiplicative error of 1 ± ε. Our algorithm runs in time ε−2 4k(n+m) poly(k). The algorithm is based on associating with each vertex an element in the exterior (or, Grassmann) algebra, called an extensor, and then performing computations in this algebra. This connection to exterior algebra generalizes a number of previous approaches for the longest path problem and is of independent conceptual interest. Using this approach, we also obtain a deterministic 2k·poly(n) time algorithm to find a k-path in a given directed graph that is promised to have few of them. Our results and techniques generalize to the subgraph isomorphism problem when the subgraphs we are looking for have bounded pathwidth. Finally, we also obtain a randomized algorithm to detect k-multilinear terms in a multivariate polynomial given as a general algebraic circuit. To the best of our knowledge, this was previously only known for algebraic circuits not involving negative constants.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Brand, Cornelius","Dell, Holger","Husfeldt, Thore"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383879"],"event":{"acronym":"STOC","title":"ACM Symposium on Theory of Computing"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Saarland University"],"keyword":["Exterior Algebra","Graph algorithms","Multivariate Polynomials","Path Counting","Subgraph Isomorphism"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"extensor coding","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/83633902/1804.09448.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},"id":"S01-000.00-383879","identifier":[{"id":"85049880689","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-b36fd584-0dce-4cf2-b49f-80c6ddcb3b91"],"keyword":["Exterior Algebra","Graph Algorithms","Multivariate Polynomials","Path Counting","Subgraph Isomorphism"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-53FAF49F787AD61120E68BEEDDB0CF62"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"https://opendatacommons.org/licenses/by/1-0/index.html","type":"rem","url":"http://arxiv.org/abs/1804.09448.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/83633902/1804.09448.pdf","version":"Pre-print"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"de","key":"saarland university","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Saarland University","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Algorithms"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Cornelius","key":"brandpau","last":"Brand","name":"Brand, Cornelius","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Holger","key":"dellpau","last":"Dell","name":"Dell, Holger","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Thore","key":"husfeldtpau","last":"Husfeldt","locid":"472021c7-73cc-44ba-a4ef-70cd2bed8601","name":"Husfeldt, Thore","orcid":"0000-0001-9078-4512","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3188745.3188902","eisbn":["9781450355599"],"pages":"151","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","subtitle":"STOC 2018","title":"Proceedings of the 50th Annual ACM SIGACT Symposium on Theory of Computing"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-b36fd584-0dce-4cf2-b49f-80c6ddcb3b91"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Extensor-coding","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383879","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"We reduce the problem of detecting the existence of an object to the problem of computing the parity of the number of objects in question. In particular, when given any non-empty set system, we prove that randomly restricting elements of its ground set makes the size of the restricted set system an odd number with significant probability. When compared to previously known reductions of this type, ours excel in their simplicity: For graph problems, restricting elements of the ground set usually corresponds to simple deletion and contraction operations, which can be encoded efficiently in most problems. We find three applications of our reductions: 1. An exponential-time algorithm: We show how to decide Hamiltonicity in directed n-vertex graphs with running time 1.9999^n provided that the graph has at most 1.0385^n Hamiltonian cycles. We do so by reducing to the algorithm of Björklund and Husfeldt (FOCS 2013) that computes the parity of the number of Hamiltonian cycles in time 1.619^n. 2. A new result in the framework of Cygan et al. (CCC 2012) for analyzing the complexity of NP-hard problems under the Strong Exponential Time Hypothesis: If the parity of the number of Set Covers can be determined in time 1.9999^n, then Set Cover can be decided in the same time. 3. A structural result in parameterized complexity: We define the pa- rameterized complexity class ⊕W[1] and prove that it is at least as hard as W[1] under randomized fpt-reductions with bounded one- sided error; this is analogous to the classical result NP ⊆ RP⊕P by Toda (SICOMP 1991).","author_count":3,"contributors":["Björklund, Andreas","Dell, Holger","Husfeldt, Thore"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382621"],"event":{"acronym":"ICALP","end":"2015-07-10","place":"Kyoto, jp","start":"2015-07-06","title":"The 42nd International Colloquium on Automata, Languages, and Programming","year":"2015"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Germany","Sweden"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Lund University","Saarland University"],"keyword":["Exponential Time Algorithms","Hamiltonicity","Parameterized complexity","Parity Computation","Set System Restrictions"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the parity of set systems under random restrictions with applications to exponential time problems","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/81821968/pbdm.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382621","identifier":[{"id":"84950138716","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-5b261d61-818a-4a29-81f5-d9466e9714de"],"keyword":["Exponential-Time Algorithms","Hamiltonicity","Parameterized Complexity","Parity Computation","Set System Restrictions"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-9E8C296B52BF54B332FAA71D74DA20EE"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/81821968/pbdm.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2015","yearSub":"2015"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"se","key":"lund university","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Lund University","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"de","key":"saarland university","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Saarland University","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Algorithms","The Department, Theoretical Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Andreas","key":"björklundpau","last":"Björklund","name":"Björklund, Andreas","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Holger","key":"dellpau","last":"Dell","name":"Dell, Holger","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Thore","key":"husfeldtpau","last":"Husfeldt","locid":"472021c7-73cc-44ba-a4ef-70cd2bed8601","name":"Husfeldt, Thore","orcid":"0000-0001-9078-4512","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-662-47672-7_19","eisbn":["9783662476727"],"isbn":["9783662476710"],"issn":["03029743"],"pages":"231-242","publisher":"Springer","series":"Lecture Notes in Computer Science","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the 42nd International Colloquium on Automata, Languages, and Programming, ICALP 2015, (6-10 July 2015, Kyoto, Japan)"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84950138716"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-5b261d61-818a-4a29-81f5-d9466e9714de"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"The Parity of Set Systems under Random Restrictions with Applications to Exponential Time Problems","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382621","year":"2015","yearsub":"2015"}
{"abstract":"We show conditional lower bounds for well-studied #P-hard problems: The number of satisfying assignments of a 2-CNF formula with n variables cannot be computed in time exp(o(n)), and the same is true for computing the number of all independent sets in an n-vertex graph. The permanent of an n× n matrix with entries 0 and 1 cannot be computed in time exp(o(n)). The Tutte polynomial of an n-vertex multigraph cannot be computed in time exp(o(n)) at most evaluation points (x,y) in the case of multigraphs, and it cannot be computed in time exp(o(n/poly log n)) in the case of simple graphs. Our lower bounds are relative to (variants of) the Exponential Time Hypothesis (ETH), which says that the satisfiability of n-variable 3-CNF formulas cannot be decided in time exp(o(n)). We relax this hypothesis by introducing its counting version #ETH; namely, that the satisfying assignments cannot be counted in time exp(o(n)). In order to use #ETH for our lower bounds, we transfer the sparsification lemma for d-CNF formulas to the counting setting.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Dell, Holger","Husfeldt, Thore","Marx, Dániel","Taslaman, Nina Sofia","Wahlén, Martin"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381446"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Germany","Sweden"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Humboldt University of Berlin","Lund University"],"keyword":["Conditional lower bounds","ETH counting version of ETH","Exponential Time Hypothesis ETH","P hard problems","Sparsification lemma"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"exponential time complexity of the permanent and the tutte polynomial","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/84906849708","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-381446","identifier":[{"id":"84906849708","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-0c67d9dd-eb0a-40bf-b565-073d93d06193"],"keyword":["#ETH (counting version of ETH)","#P-hard problems","Conditional lower bounds","Exponential Time Hypothesis (ETH)","Sparsification lemma"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-0AC01895D6543044A266D7231408E51D"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2014","yearSub":"2014"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"de","key":"humboldt university of berlin","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Humboldt University of Berlin","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Algorithms","The Department, Theoretical Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"se","key":"lund university","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Lund University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Holger","key":"dellpau","last":"Dell","name":"Dell, Holger","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Thore","key":"husfeldtpau","last":"Husfeldt","locid":"472021c7-73cc-44ba-a4ef-70cd2bed8601","name":"Husfeldt, Thore","orcid":"0000-0001-9078-4512","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Dániel","key":"marxpau","last":"Marx","name":"Marx, Dániel","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Nina Sofia","key":"taslamanpau","last":"Taslaman","name":"Taslaman, Nina Sofia","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Martin","key":"wahlénpau","last":"Wahlén","name":"Wahlén, Martin","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/2635812","issn":["15496325","15496333"],"issn_org":["15496325"],"issue":"4","journal":"ACM Transactions on Algorithms","paperid":"21","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"ACM Transactions on Algorithms","title_org":"A C M Transactions on Algorithms","volume":"10"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-0c67d9dd-eb0a-40bf-b565-073d93d06193"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Exponential Time Complexity of the Permanent and the Tutte Polynomial","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381446","year":"2014","yearsub":"2014"}
{"abstract":"The Valiant-Vazirani Isolation Lemma provides an efficient procedure for isolating a satisfying assignment of a given satisfiable circuit: Given a Boolean circuit C on n input variables, the procedure outputs a new circuit C' on the same n input variables such that (i) every satisfying assignment of C' also satisfies C, and (ii) if C is satisfiable, then C' has exactly one satisfying assignment. In particular, if C is unsatisfiable, then (i) implies that C' is unsatisfiable. The Valiant-Vazirani procedure is randomized, and when C is satisfiable it produces a uniquely satisfiable circuit C' with probability Omega(1/n). Is it possible to have an efficient deterministic witness-isolating procedure? Or, at least, is it possible to improve the success probability of a randomized procedure to a large constant? We argue that the answer is likely `No'. More precisely, we prove that there exists a non-uniform randomized polynomial-time witness-isolating procedure with success probability bigger than 2/3 if and only if NP is in P/poly. Thus, an improved witness-isolating procedure would imply the collapse of the polynomial-time hierarchy. We establish similar results for other variants of witness isolation, such as reductions that remove all but an odd number of satisfying assignments of a satisfiable circuit. We also consider a black box setting of witness isolation that generalizes the setting of the Valiant-Vazirani Isolation Lemma, and give an upper bound of O(1/n) on the success probability for a natural class of randomized witness-isolating procedures.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Dell, Holger","Kabanets, Valentine","van Melkebeek, Dieter","Watanabe, Osamu"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-695229"],"filters":{"added":"2023-11","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Asia, East","OECD"],"country":["Canada","Japan","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Simon Fraser University","Tokyo Institute of Technology","University of Wisconsin-Madison"],"keyword":["Derandomization","Isolation Lemma","Parity Satisfiability","Unique Satisfiability"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"is valiant vazirani s isolation probability improvable","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/101448599/Revision2OfTR11-151.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-695229","ids":["itu-2060335e-5efe-4f36-a96d-9a894b185809"],"keyword":["Derandomization","Isolation Lemma","Parity Satisfiability","Unique Satisfiability"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-9342548EF60B2E034CE01DC6E2EADDAC"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/101448599/Revision2OfTR11-151.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Algorithms"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"ca","key":"simon fraser university","loc":["Canada","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Simon Fraser University","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"us","key":"university of wisconsin madison","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of Wisconsin-Madison","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"jp","key":"tokyo institute of technology","loc":["Japan","Asia, East","OECD"],"name":"Tokyo Institute of Technology","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Holger","key":"dellpau","last":"Dell","locid":"ef9b447a-d6c4-4250-a506-093730db84d6","name":"Dell, Holger","orcid":"0000-0001-8955-0786","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Valentine","key":"kabanetspau","last":"Kabanets","name":"Kabanets, Valentine","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Dieter","key":"vanmelkebeekpau","last":"van Melkebeek","name":"van Melkebeek, Dieter","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Osamu","key":"watanabepau","last":"Watanabe","name":"Watanabe, Osamu","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"X"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Undefined"]},"sid":["itu-2060335e-5efe-4f36-a96d-9a894b185809"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Is Valiant–Vazirani’s isolation probability improvable?","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-695229","year":"2013","yearsub":"2013"}
{"abstract":"BACKGROUND: Bivalirudin, as compared with heparin and glycoprotein IIb/IIIa inhibitors, has been shown to reduce rates of bleeding and death in patients undergoing primary percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI). Whether these benefits persist in contemporary practice characterized by prehospital initiation of treatment, optional use of glycoprotein IIb/IIIa inhibitors and novel P2Y 12 inhibitors, and radial-artery PCI access use is unknown. METHODS: We randomly assigned 2218 patients with ST-segment elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI) who were being transported for primary PCI to receive either bivalirudin or unfractionated or low-molecular-weight heparin with optional glycoprotein IIb/IIIa inhibitors (control group). The primary outcome at 30 days was a composite of death or major bleeding not associated with coronary-artery bypass grafting (CABG), and the principal secondary outcome was a composite of death, reinfarction, or non-CABG major bleeding. RESULTS: Bivalirudin, as compared with the control intervention, reduced the risk of the primary outcome (5.1% vs. 8.5%; relative risk, 0.60; 95% confidence interval [CI], 0.43 to 0.82; P=0.001) and the principal secondary outcome (6.6% vs. 9.2%; relative risk, 0.72; 95% CI, 0.54 to 0.96; P=0.02). Bivalirudin also reduced the risk of major bleeding (2.6% vs. 6.0%; relative risk, 0.43; 95% CI, 0.28 to 0.66; P<0.001). The risk of acute stent thrombosis was higher with bivalirudin (1.1% vs. 0.2%; relative risk, 6.11; 95% CI, 1.37 to 27.24; P=0.007). There was no significant difference in rates of death (2.9% vs. 3.1%) or reinfarction (1.7% vs. 0.9%). Results were consistent across subgroups of patients. CONCLUSIONS: Bivalirudin, started during transport for primary PCI, improved 30-day clinical outcomes with a reduction in major bleeding but with an increase in acute stent thrombosis.","author_count":28,"contributors":["Steg, Philippe Gabriel","van 't Hof, Arnoud","Hamm, Christian W","Clemmensen, Peter"